WEBVTT

00:00:14.634 --> 00:00:29.325
Cheedeb presents the celebrated jumping frog of Calaveras County and Curing a Cold by Mark Twain.

00:00:29.385 --> 00:00:36.270
The California Gold Rush.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:49.846
On January 24th, 1848, John Marshall discovered gold in the American River at Sutter's Fort, California.

00:00:49.906 --> 00:00:53.079
He wasn't looking for gold.

00:00:53.138 --> 00:00:57.202
He discovered it by chance.

00:00:57.262 --> 00:01:03.476
This discovery changed the destiny of California.

00:01:03.537 --> 00:01:23.864
Suddenly, this wild territory of the far west became world-famous During 1849, more than 80,000 people arrived in California looking for gold.

00:01:23.924 --> 00:01:32.712
They came from the United States, Europe, Central and South America, and China.

00:01:32.771 --> 00:01:43.596
They were called gold miners, gold prospectors, or simply 49ers.

00:01:43.655 --> 00:01:47.974
A lot of 49ers were poor.

00:01:48.034 --> 00:01:54.640
Their dream was to find gold and strike it rich.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:02:03.016
A lot of them found gold, but only a few became very rich and important.

00:02:03.076 --> 00:02:14.420
With the hard-working gold miners, there came saloon keepers, gamblers, dishonest people, and outlaws.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:25.981
It was difficult to keep law and order in the far west.

00:02:26.041 --> 00:02:37.056
The celebrated jumping frog of Calaveras County.

00:02:37.116 --> 00:02:41.044
A letter from the east.

00:02:41.104 --> 00:02:47.422
One day a friend of mine from the east wrote me a letter.

00:02:47.481 --> 00:02:53.934
He asked me to visit an old man named Simon Wheeler.

00:02:53.993 --> 00:03:03.496
My friend wanted me to ask Simon Wheeler about a childhood companion, Leonidas W.

00:03:03.556 --> 00:03:05.257
Smiley.

00:03:05.316 --> 00:03:15.883
I found Simon Wheeler sleeping by the stove of the old tavern in Angel's mining camp.

00:03:15.943 --> 00:03:20.868
I noticed that he was fat.

00:03:20.928 --> 00:03:24.672
His face was simple and peaceful.

00:03:24.732 --> 00:03:35.301
When he woke up, I told him that a friend of mine wanted to know about a childhood companion named Leonidas W.

00:03:35.361 --> 00:03:36.953
Smiley.

00:03:37.013 --> 00:03:43.258
Simon Wheeler blocked me with his chair in a corner of the tavern.

00:03:43.318 --> 00:03:50.062
Then, very seriously, he began to tell me the story that follows.

00:03:50.122 --> 00:03:56.506
There was once a fellow at the mining camp named Jim Smiley.

00:03:56.566 --> 00:04:01.138
It was the winter of 1849, or maybe the spring of 1850.

00:04:01.158 --> 00:04:05.077
He was an unusual man.

00:04:05.137 --> 00:04:15.420
He always bet money on anything that happened at the mining camp, and he usually won.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:19.844
Jim Smiley was a very lucky man.

00:04:19.904 --> 00:04:31.452
When there was a horse race, a dog fight, a cat fight, or a chicken fight, he always bet on them.

00:04:31.512 --> 00:04:41.018
Exercise 4 Now, listen and check your answers.

00:04:41.078 --> 00:04:47.122
Dear Mark, do you still remember me?

00:04:47.182 --> 00:04:51.864
I'm William Brown, your friend from New York.

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:56.596
I want to go to California to look for gold.

00:04:56.656 --> 00:05:03.038
My dream is to live in a mining camp in the Sierra Nevada mountains.

00:05:03.098 --> 00:05:10.350
Can you go to Angel's mining camp to visit an old man named Simon Wheeler?

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:15.592
Simon knows my childhood friend, Leonidas W.

00:05:15.652 --> 00:05:17.072
Smiley.

00:05:17.132 --> 00:05:24.746
I want to know all about Leonidas Smiley before I travel to California.

00:05:24.806 --> 00:05:27.407
Thank you, Mark.

00:05:27.467 --> 00:05:32.137
Your friend, William.

00:05:32.197 --> 00:05:37.206
bets and races.

00:05:37.266 --> 00:05:44.558
Smiley owned an old horse that was slow and sick, but he won money on her.

00:05:44.618 --> 00:05:50.589
The poor horse had asthma and other animal illnesses.

00:05:50.649 --> 00:05:56.300
At races she had a head start because of her health problems.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:02.476
Near the end of every race She became very excited and desperate.

00:06:02.536 --> 00:06:19.000
She galloped wildly, kicked her legs in the air to the sides, raised the dust, coughed and blew her nose.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:24.770
At the end of the race, she was all's the happy winner.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:31.418
Smiley also had a small bulldog named Andrew Jackson.

00:06:31.478 --> 00:06:41.734
Andrew Jackson was a lazy dog and did nothing all day long, but when Smiley bet money on him, he was at lively and ready to fight.

00:06:41.794 --> 00:06:52.845
When there was an important dogfight at the mining camp, Andrew Jackson always grabbed the back leg of the other dog, He didn't chew on it.

00:06:52.905 --> 00:06:55.886
He only kept it between his strong teeth.

00:06:55.946 --> 00:07:03.077
He didn't let go until Smiley won the bet.

00:07:03.137 --> 00:07:21.114
Life in the Mining Camps From 1848, many mining camps were established in the Gold Country, near the American River and in the Sierra Nevada Mountains.

00:07:21.174 --> 00:07:25.312
These camps were small settlements.

00:07:25.372 --> 00:07:31.540
They were organized quickly to meet the miners' everyday needs.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:44.428
A mining camp usually had a saloon, a hotel, a stable, a general store, an undertaker, and a bank.

00:07:44.488 --> 00:07:54.820
Miners often paid for their food, drink, clothing, and other things with gold dust or gold nuggets.

00:07:54.880 --> 00:08:01.996
Brawls and fights were common and killings were not unusual.

00:08:02.056 --> 00:08:09.222
These were tough times and camps were rough places.

00:08:09.282 --> 00:08:15.448
Today, a lot of camps have become ghost towns.

00:08:15.508 --> 00:08:35.876
Others have grown into towns and cities such as Angels Camp, Auburn, Grass Valley, Jackson, Mary Posa, Nevada City, Murphy's, Placerville, and Sonora.

00:08:35.936 --> 00:08:42.114
You can visit them and their abandoned mines when you go to California.

00:08:42.174 --> 00:08:43.790
take U.S.

00:08:43.850 --> 00:08:54.693
State Highway 80 to Sacramento and then take California Highway 49 to the Gold Country.

00:08:54.753 --> 00:08:59.164
Daniel Webster.

00:08:59.224 --> 00:09:06.436
Smiley found a frog one day and named it Daniel Webster.

00:09:06.496 --> 00:09:11.761
He took it home and planned to teach it a lot of things.

00:09:11.821 --> 00:09:20.538
For three months, Smiley sat outside his house and taught his frog how to jump far.

00:09:20.598 --> 00:09:25.461
Daniel Webster was a clever frog and learned quickly.

00:09:25.521 --> 00:09:33.134
Smiley gave him a little push from behind and Daniel Webster flew into the air like a doughnut.

00:09:33.194 --> 00:09:38.218
He even turned one or two somersaults in the air.

00:09:38.278 --> 00:09:42.384
Smiley taught his frog to catch flies.

00:09:42.444 --> 00:09:46.368
Flies Daniel flies, smiley shouted.

00:09:46.428 --> 00:09:51.340
Daniel jumped up and caught the fly with his long tongue.

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:57.370
Daniel Webster was the champion jumping frog of Calaveras County.

00:09:57.430 --> 00:10:01.331
Smiley won all bets with his handsome frog.

00:10:01.391 --> 00:10:06.762
He was very proud of Daniel Webster.

00:10:06.822 --> 00:10:11.203
Do you have a pet frog?

00:10:11.263 --> 00:10:18.261
If you do, Take it to the Jumping Frog Jubilee.

00:10:18.321 --> 00:10:27.536
Every year in May at Angels Camp, California, there is an exciting frog competition.

00:10:27.596 --> 00:10:32.310
It is called the Jumping Frog Jubilee.

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:37.452
Frogs from far and near can enter the competition.

00:10:37.512 --> 00:10:42.675
The frog that can jump the greatest distance is the winner.

00:10:42.735 --> 00:10:50.370
A frog named Rosie the Ribberter is now the jumping champion of the world.

00:10:50.430 --> 00:11:01.918
In May 1986, she jumped 21 feet, five and three-quarter inches, about seven meters.

00:11:01.978 --> 00:11:17.482
If you want more information, write to Calaveras Jumping Frog Jubilee Post Office Box 489, Angels Camp, California 95222.

00:11:17.542 --> 00:11:18.773
Fax, 209-736-2476.

00:11:18.793 --> 00:11:30.470
A Stranger at Angels Camp.

00:11:30.530 --> 00:11:44.887
Smiley kept his frog in a box with small holes in it.

00:11:44.947 --> 00:11:49.678
One day, a stranger came to the mining camp.

00:11:49.738 --> 00:11:52.829
He saw Smiley holding the box.

00:11:52.889 --> 00:11:54.700
What's in that box?

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:56.720
asked the stranger.

00:11:56.780 --> 00:12:00.502
Maybe a parrot, maybe a canary.

00:12:00.562 --> 00:12:04.932
No, it's a frog, answered Smiley.

00:12:04.993 --> 00:12:09.290
The stranger took the box and looked at it carefully.

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:11.176
Yes, it is.

00:12:11.236 --> 00:12:13.897
Well, what's he good for?

00:12:13.957 --> 00:12:18.489
Well, smiley said, he's good for one thing.

00:12:18.549 --> 00:12:23.362
He can out jump any frog in Calaveras County.

00:12:23.422 --> 00:12:26.613
Stranger took the box again.

00:12:26.673 --> 00:12:30.305
Looked at the frog for a long time.

00:12:30.365 --> 00:12:36.968
Then he returned it to smiley and said, I don't see anything special about this frog.

00:12:37.028 --> 00:12:40.360
Maybe you don't, said Smiley.

00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:45.713
Maybe you understand frogs, and maybe you don't understand them.

00:12:45.773 --> 00:12:52.208
But I'll bet $40 that he can out-jump any frog in Calaver's County."

00:12:52.268 --> 00:12:54.338
The fellow answered.

00:12:54.398 --> 00:12:59.993
Well, I'm only a stranger here, and I don't have a frog.

00:13:00.052 --> 00:13:01.584
I can't bet.

00:13:01.644 --> 00:13:02.954
That's all right.

00:13:03.014 --> 00:13:04.219
That's all right.

00:13:04.279 --> 00:13:06.224
Hold my box a minute.

00:13:06.284 --> 00:13:11.476
I'll go to the river and find a frog for you," said Smiley.

00:13:11.536 --> 00:13:16.328
The stranger took the box and bet his forty dollars.

00:13:16.388 --> 00:13:21.552
Smiley bet his forty dollars too and went to the river.

00:13:21.612 --> 00:13:26.254
The stranger sat down and waited for Smiley.

00:13:26.314 --> 00:13:32.518
He sat there a long time and he began to think.

00:13:32.578 --> 00:13:38.854
He took Daniel Webster out of the box and opened his mouth.

00:13:38.914 --> 00:13:44.618
With a teaspoon, he put some shotgun pellets into the frog's mouth.

00:13:44.678 --> 00:13:48.582
Then he put him on the floor.

00:13:48.642 --> 00:14:02.033
Panning for gold The expression panning for gold was born during the California gold rush in 1848.

00:14:02.093 --> 00:14:08.247
The miners used a pan with a wire net on the bottom.

00:14:08.307 --> 00:14:15.060
They separated the gold from other materials by washing them with water.

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:21.142
Miners usually pan for gold in the water of the rivers.

00:14:21.202 --> 00:14:33.274
The water of the river filled their pan with sand, dirt, stones, and perhaps gold dust or gold nuggets.

00:14:33.334 --> 00:14:43.018
Other miners looked for gold in caves in the Sierra Nevada Mountains, and they used a pickaxe.

00:14:43.078 --> 00:14:49.363
These caves often became gold mines.

00:14:49.424 --> 00:14:53.376
The big bet.

00:14:53.436 --> 00:15:06.430
After some time, Smiley returned, holding a frog, and said, Now put him next to Daniel with his front paws in the same place.

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:08.502
I'll give the word."

00:15:08.562 --> 00:15:18.072
Then smiley said, one, two, three, go.

00:15:18.132 --> 00:15:21.925
He and the stranger pushed the frogs from behind.

00:15:21.985 --> 00:15:26.698
The new frog jumped forward, but Daniel didn't budge.

00:15:26.758 --> 00:15:31.418
He tried to move his shoulders, but he was anchored to the floor.

00:15:31.478 --> 00:15:35.480
Smiley was very surprised and unhappy.

00:15:35.540 --> 00:15:38.212
The stranger won the bet.

00:15:38.272 --> 00:15:43.636
Well, he said, I don't see anything special about this frog.

00:15:43.696 --> 00:15:46.718
He took the money and left.

00:15:46.778 --> 00:15:50.510
Smiley looked at Daniel for a long time.

00:15:50.570 --> 00:15:55.171
Finally, he said, what has happened to Daniel?

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:58.060
He looks terribly fat.

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:02.123
He caught Daniel by the back of his neck and lifted him.

00:16:02.182 --> 00:16:03.784
Cats alive!

00:16:03.844 --> 00:16:06.646
You weigh five pounds!

00:16:06.706 --> 00:16:14.630
He turned Daniel upside down, and the frog spit out a big number of shotgun pellets.

00:16:14.690 --> 00:16:17.942
Now he understood what happened to Daniel.

00:16:18.002 --> 00:16:20.314
Smiley was furious.

00:16:20.374 --> 00:16:25.352
He looked for the stranger everywhere, but he never found him.

00:16:25.412 --> 00:16:28.808
Simon Wheeler's story ended here.

00:16:28.868 --> 00:16:38.630
I didn't really want to hear another story about Smiley's best, so I got up from my chair and left.

00:16:38.690 --> 00:16:42.950
